# Test environment for `bun test` in app/api.
#
# Bun automatically sets NODE_ENV=test and loads this file (as `.env.test`)
# when running `bun test` from this directory — no shell exports needed:
#
#   cp .env.test.example .env.test
#
# Every value here is a safe, non-secret placeholder for unit tests only.
# Unit tests never connect to Postgres or Redis; the URLs below just satisfy
# the configuration contract's format validation. Production keeps its strict
# validation (https origins, real OAuth providers) — this file cannot weaken
# it because it is only read under NODE_ENV=test.

# ── Auth (Better Auth) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ≥32 chars, not a well-known placeholder. Never reuse a real secret here.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=test-only-secret-not-valid-in-production-0123456789abcdef
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3001

# ── Database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Format-validation only; unit tests do not open connections.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/abugida_test

# ── OAuth provider (required in every environment) ────────────────────────
# `@abugida/auth` refuses to build an auth instance without at least one
# OAuth provider, in every environment. Telegram OIDC is the repo's primary
# provider (tests such as tests/openapi-document.test.ts assert the
# Telegram endpoints exist), so the placeholders configure Telegram. They
# are not real credentials and never hit Telegram's endpoints — unit tests
# do not perform OAuth flows.
TELEGRAM_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=test-only-telegram-client-id
TELEGRAM_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=test-only-telegram-secret-not-used-in-production
